            "text": "At dawn, the city sounds unfinished. A truck groans somewhere beyond the apartment walls, pipes answer with a soft knock, and pigeons rearrange themselves on the roof like thoughts seeking order. I stand by the window with a mug of tea, watching one pale stripe of sunlight climb the opposite building.\n\nA woman in a yellow coat appears below. She walks slowly, carrying a paper bag that swings at her side. I wonder what is inside: bread, perhaps, or oranges, or some small private plan for the day. At the corner, a bus exhales and opens its doors. She steps aboard, and the city closes around her.\n\nThere is comfort in not knowing. We are surrounded by thousands of lives, each carrying invisible weather. Someone is remembering a song. Someone else is deciding to leave. A child is pressing a forehead against glass, amazed by a dog wearing a red sweater.\n\nThe morning brightens. My tea cools. On the sill, a plant leans toward the light, its newest leaf imperfect and brave. I touch the soil, though it does not need me. Perhaps that is the lesson: care is not always rescue. Sometimes it is simply attention, offered quietly, without proof that anything will change.\n\nOutside, a cyclist rings a bell. The sound travels upward, bright as a coin. I open the window. Cold air enters, carrying rain, bread, exhaust, and the faint green scent of spring.\n\nFor a moment, the world seems possible\u2014not perfect, but possible."
          }
